nd various utilities scattered across rel04.1 through rel05.4 that haven't been started yet.\n\nThe issue is that the requirement_states only includes SRDs from srd004-ts onward and srd034+, which means either those earlier SRDs' requirements are already complete or they haven't been set up for tracking in this generation run. Since the code_status shows cat as \"not started,\" it seems the requirement_states might only be tracking SRDs that have been prepared for this particular run.\n\nGiven the constraint to only propose tasks for R-items with \"ready\" status, I need to focus on the SRDs listed in requirement_states. Looking at the earliest incomplete release, I can see that srd034 through srd038 all have ready requirements starting from rel06.0, with varying ranges of ready items across each SRD. I'm noticing that srd004-ts appears late in rel15.0, so I should deprioritize it. Based on the release timeline, I'll tackle rel06.0 first with its five core utilities, then move through rel06.1, rel07.0, and rel07.1 in sequence. The challenge is that I need to verify the weight budgets from requirements.yaml, but I don't have access to that file and can only work with what's in the project context.\n\nSince the requirement states show status but not weights, I'll assume the default weight of 1 for each requirement. This means I can fit up to 4 requirements per task while staying within the maximum weight limit. For rel06.0, I need to organize the remaining requirements for mkdir across multiple tasks, keeping each task to 4 requirements or fewer. I'm weighing whether to spread the mkdir requirements across three tasks or combine them with starting the rmdir work. The second approach makes more sense—I can fit all the mkdir groups into three tasks and use the fourth task to begin rmdir, which gives better coverage across the release timeline.\n\nBut I realize I need to include the SRD file references in the task descriptions so the stitch agent can read them independently without relying on my analysis. I'm also reconsidering the contract task requirement—for cmd/ packages like mkdir and rmdir, the main.go itself serves as the contract since these are standalone binaries, not libraries. The contract constraint seems more relevant for pkg/ packages that export types and interfaces.\n\nNow I'm laying out my four tasks: the first handles parent directory creation with the -p flag, the second covers mode setting with -m, and I'm working through the remaining requirements to distribute them appropriately across the task weight limits.\n\nI should make sure these task descriptions reference the actual SRD file so the stitch agent can look up what each requirement means. I'm also thinking through potential dependencies between tasks.\n\nSince tasks 0, 1, and 2 all modify the same mkdir file, they need to run sequentially to avoid conflicts. Task 3 is independent since it's a new utility. So the dependency chain should be: Task 0 has no dependencies, Task 1 depends on Task 0, Task 2 depends on Task 1, and Task 3 stands alone. I'm realizing I need to respect the 4-requirement limit per task and the 4-task maximum, which means I can handle at most 16 requirements total. Since mkdir has 10 remaining requirements (R2.1-R4.3), I should prioritize finishing it first according to the P1 principle of focusing on the earliest incomplete release. Let me map out the SRD file paths for mkdir and rmdir, then craft thorough task descriptions that are self-contained.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the file structure each task will touch—mkdir tasks modify the main implementation and tests, while rmdir would create new files entirely. But P2 requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, which would demand 6 tasks just for mkdir alone. That conflicts with the 4-task limit, so the hard constraint on task count takes precedence.\n\nLooking at recent commit patterns, it seems implementation and tests are bundled together in single tasks anyway, so I'll follow that established approach rather than strictly separating them.\n\nNow I need to map out the four tasks properly. For mkdir, the remaining requirements likely break down into parent directory creation with the -p flag, mode setting with -m, and error handling for edge cases. For rmdir, I'm looking at basic removal, the --ignore-fail-on-non-empty option, and the -p flag for removing parent directories.\n\nLet me structure this as four concrete tasks: parent creation for mkdir, mode setting, error handling, and basic rmdir removal. I'm checking the weight of each task against the four-requirement limit—the first three tasks for mkdir come in at three, four, and three requirements respectively, which all fit within the constraint.\n\nThe dependency chain flows logically: the parent creation task builds on existing code, mode setting depends on that foundation, error handling builds on both previous tasks, and rmdir is independent. Now I need to flesh out each task with the standard cmd package structure including main.go, test files, and references to the SRD requirements and testing utilities.\n\nI should be careful to reference requirement IDs without reproducing the actual SRD text, letting the stitch agent handle those details. The task titles should follow the established format with the stitch prefix, command name, brief description, and SRD reference with requirement IDs.\n\nFor the task descriptions, I need to structure them with deliverable type, required reading, files to modify, requirement mappings, design decisions from the SRD, and clear acceptance criteria that are verifiable. Since I don't have the full requirement details, I'll focus on what the stitch agent needs to understand about what to implement, and they can fill in specifics from the SRD itself. I'm realizing the constraint to split implementation and tests into separate tasks conflicts with covering all the requirements in just 4 tasks. If I split each feature area, I'd only